.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt DHCLIENT 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm dhclient
.Nd Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) client
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl bdrv
.Op Fl l Ar leasefile
.Op Fl p Ar pidfile
.Ar interface
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
gets an IPv4 address and network configuration for
.Ar interface
from a DHCP server, applies it, and keeps it: it renews the lease before it
runs out, and takes the configuration away if it does.
One
.Nm
runs per interface; at boot
.Pa /etc/rc.d/netif
starts it for each interface whose
.Va ifconfig_ Ns Ar interface
in
.Xr rc.conf 5
is
.Dq DHCP .
.Pp
It follows RFC 2131.
It broadcasts a DHCPDISCOVER, requests the first acceptable offer, and once
the server acknowledges it is bound.
Halfway through the lease (the server's renewal time, T1) it asks the
server that granted the lease to extend it; at seven eighths (T2) it asks any
server; and if the lease expires all the same, it removes the address and
starts again.
A lease saved by an earlier run that hasn't expired is asked for again first.
Requests are sent again after 4, 8, 16, 32 and then every 64 seconds, each
time one second more or less at random.
.Pp
A lease gives the interface its address and mask, sets the default route
to the lease's first router, and rewrites
.Pa /etc/resolv.conf
with the lease's name servers and domain (a lease without name servers
leaves it as it is).
The domain name and host name a server sends are used only if they are
plain names: one with any other character is ignored.
The client identifier sent is the interface's Ethernet address.
.Pp
Unless
.Fl d
or
.Fl b
is given,
.Nm
stays in the foreground until it is bound, or for 60 seconds, so that the
boot goes on with the network configured, and then goes into the
background.
It logs through
.Xr syslog 3
with the
.Dv daemon
facility, and also to the standard error while in the foreground.
SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP make it exit, leaving the interface configured
and the lease saved.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl b
Go into the background at once.
.It Fl d
Stay in the foreground.
.It Fl l Ar leasefile
Save the lease in
.Ar leasefile
instead of
.Pa /var/db/dhclient.leases. Ns Ar interface .
.It Fl p Ar pidfile
Write the process ID to
.Ar pidfile
instead of
.Pa /var/run/dhclient/dhclient. Ns Ar interface Ns Pa .pid .
.It Fl r
On exit, send the server a DHCPRELEASE and remove the address.
.It Fl v
Also log every message ignored, and why.
.El
.Pp
Only the superuser may run
.Nm .
A second
.Nm
for the same interface finds the first's pid file locked and exits.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/run/dhclient/dhclient.<if>.pid" -compact
.It Pa /var/db/dhclient.leases. Ns Ar interface
The lease, in FreeBSD's layout:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lease {
  interface "rl0";
  fixed-address 10.0.2.15;
  option subnet-mask 255.255.255.0;
  option routers 10.0.2.2;
  option domain-name-servers 10.0.2.3;
  option dhcp-lease-time 86400;
  option dhcp-renewal-time 43200;
  option dhcp-rebinding-time 75600;
  option dhcp-server-identifier 10.0.2.2;
  renew 1 2026/10/19 09:00:00;
  rebind 1 2026/10/19 18:00:00;
  expire 1 2026/10/19 21:00:00;
}
.Ed
The times are UTC, after the day of the week (0 is Sunday).
.It Pa /var/run/dhclient/dhclient. Ns Ar interface Ns Pa .pid
The process ID of the client for
.Ar interface .
.It Pa /etc/resolv.conf
Written from the lease, all at once.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 when it is told to stop, and 1 if it can't start.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Under QEMU:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# dhclient -d rl0
DHCPDISCOVER on rl0 to 255.255.255.255 port 67 interval 4
DHCPOFFER from 10.0.2.2
DHCPREQUEST on rl0 to 255.255.255.255 port 67
DHCPACK from 10.0.2.2
bound to 10.0.2.15 -- renewal in 43200 seconds.
.Ed
.Pp
In
.Xr rc.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ifconfig_rl0="DHCP"
.Ed
.Pp
and by hand, through its
.Pa rc.d
script:
.Dl # service dhclient restart rl0
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr resolv.conf 5 ,
.Xr ifconfig 8 ,
.Xr route 8
.Rs
.%A R. Droms
.%T Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol
.%R RFC 2131
.%D March 1997
.Re
.Rs
.%A S. Alexander
.%A R. Droms
.%T DHCP Options and BOOTP Vendor Extensions
.%R RFC 2132
.%D March 1997
.Re
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
from the ISC's DHCP distribution was in the BSDs' base systems from the late
1990s; OpenBSD rewrote it, and
.Fx 6.0
took the rewrite.
OpenBSD has since replaced it with the smaller dhcpleased.
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD: a small daemon in dhcpleased's
manner, under FreeBSD's name and file layout.
.Sh BUGS
There is no configuration file: the options asked for and the ones used are
fixed.
The offered address isn't checked with ARP before it is used, and only the
first offer is considered.
The host name a server sends is saved in the lease but never set.
